<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Mesoamerica and the Caribbean</p><h1 class="st-title">Bahamas</h1><p class="st-meta">1973 CE – present · capital Nassau</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Archipelago of some 700 islands off Florida, independent since 1973 and one of the wealthiest Caribbean states through tourism and finance.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After winning majority rule in 1967, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lynden-pindling">Lynden Pindling's</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/progressive-liberal-party">Progressive Liberal Party</a> led the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/colony-of-the-bahamas">Colony of the Bahamas</a> to independence on 10 July 1973 as the Commonwealth of The Bahamas, a parliamentary monarchy with the British monarch as head of state.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lynden-pindling">Pindling</a>, called the father of the nation, governed until 1992, expanding education and Bahamian ownership, though his government was tarnished by drug trafficking scandals in the 1980s, when the islands served as a transit route for cocaine to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-states">United States</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hubert-ingraham">Hubert Ingraham's</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/free-national-movement">Free National Movement</a> then alternated in power with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/progressive-liberal-party">Progressive Liberal Party</a>. Tourism, especially cruise and resort tourism on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-providence">New Providence</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grand-bahama">Grand Bahama</a>, and offshore banking dominate the economy.</p><h2>To the present</h2><p>The Bahamas remains a stable parliamentary democracy. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hurricane-dorian">Hurricane Dorian</a> devastated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abaco-islands">Abaco</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grand-bahama">Grand Bahama</a> in 2019, and rising sea levels threaten the low-lying islands. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-davis">Philip Davis</a> became prime minister in 2021.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/bahamas.jpg" alt="Bahamas" width="320" height="160"><figcaption>Image: Government of Bahamas · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_Bahamas.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1973 CE – present</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/21st-century">21st century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Nassau</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Lynden Pindling, Hubert Ingraham</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Protestantism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>English and Bahamian Creole</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/bahamas">Bahamas</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Commonwealth of The Bahamas</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=2000&amp;focus=bahamas" aria-label="Bahamas on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2629 1255 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Bahamas lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M2949 1511L2949 1511"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M2949 1511h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M2949 1511h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M2949 1511h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Nassau. <a href="/atlas?year=2000&amp;focus=bahamas">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
